Mitcad's patch to OCCT 8.0.1, under OCCT's licence:
SPDX-License-Identifier: LGPL-2.1-only WITH OCCT-exception-1.0

Intersections: a walked line of two parametric surfaces that ends short
of a surface's boundary is put on it within a room that grows with a
parameter range longer than a full turn, so that a surface parametrized
by arc length is treated as one parametrized by angle or over [0, 1]
(mitcad#2384, GitHub mitcad/Mitcad#641).

The case: a loft from a circle of radius 170 at z = 315 to one of radius
85 at z = 0 centred at x = 225 (an oblique cone, its side a B-spline of
degree 8 x 1 parametrized by arc length, u 0 .. 1068.14, v 0 .. 387.10),
and its mirror image about x = 0. The two sides cross in the plane x = 0
along a curve from (0, 170, 315) down to (0, 0, 142.26) and up to
(0, -170, 315); at both ends the sides share their tangent plane. The walk
from (0, 170, 315) stopped at (0, -169.9989, 314.9959), 4.2 micrometres
short of the top boundary (5e-3 in v), and PutToBoundary, whose room is a
thousandth of a parameter (aTol), did not put it on the boundary. The top
circle was not split there, the mirror image's side was not cut and was
classified inside the loft as a whole, and the union was the loft alone.
The kernel's own loft of the same circles (v over [0, 1], so a thousandth
is 0.3 mm) was put on the boundary and joined right.

- IntWalk_PWalking::PutToBoundary: an end of the line is put on a
  boundary when it lies within aTolOf(range) of it in that parameter of
  that surface: aTol for a range up to 2 pi (as before: analytic and
  [0, 1] surfaces unchanged), aTol * range / (2 pi) for a longer one.
  SeekPointOnBoundary still finds the surfaces' common point on the
  boundary, within its own tolerance, or adds none; IsParallel keeps aTol.

The patch's test and its before/after proof:
third_party/vcpkg-ports/docs/opencascade-patch-tests.md, section 2384.
